= Project Procurement Management
== Project Procurement Management

Project procurement is the process of buying or acquiring goods, services, or resources from external suppliers needed to complete a project. Project Procurement Management is the formal process of planning, acquiring, and managing these goods and services to achieve project objectives.

=== Procurement Processes in ICT Projects 
+ *Planning Purchases and Acquisitions:* Decide what hardware, software, or IT services are required, when they are needed, and how to purchase them.
+ *Planning Contracting:* Clearly define the technical and service requirements and identify suitable vendors or suppliers.
+ *Requesting Seller Responses:* Ask suppliers for quotations, bids, proposals, or product information.
+ *Selecting Sellers:* Compare suppliers based on cost, quality, technical capability, and reliability, then select the best supplier and negotiate the contract.
+ *Administering the Contract:* Monitor the supplier's work to ensure proper quality, timely delivery, and satisfactory service.
+ *Closing the Contract:* Complete the contract, make final payments, resolve any remaining issues, and confirm successful completion.

=== Bidder Selection Scheme & Criteria
A bidder selection scheme is a method used in project procurement to choose the best supplier or vendor from several bidders, balancing quality, cost, and reliability.

*Steps / Process:*
+ *Define Criteria:* Decide important factors like price, quality, delivery time, and past performance.
+ *Invite Bids:* Ask potential suppliers to submit proposals, quotations, or bids.
+ *Evaluate Bids:* Compare all bids using the defined selection criteria.
+ *Select the Best Bidder:* Choose the supplier offering the best overall value.

*Seller Selection Criteria:*
- Cost / Price (competitive pricing)
- Quality (meeting required standards)
- Delivery Time (on-time delivery)
- Experience / Expertise (track record in similar projects)
- Reliability / Reputation
- Support / Service (after-sales support and maintenance)
- Financial Stability
- Compliance (legal and regulatory standards)

=== Contract Closure Procedure
The contract closure procedure is the process of formally completing and settling a contract after all required project work has been completed.
+ *Verify Deliverables:* Check that all goods or services have been delivered according to the contract.
+ *Resolve Open Issues:* Resolve any remaining payments, disputes, defects, or incomplete work.
+ *Obtain Formal Acceptance:* Get confirmation or sign-off from the client.
+ *Finalize Financials:* Make the final payment and settle all financial matters.
+ *Document Lessons Learned:* Record what went well and what problems occurred.
+ *Archive Contract Documents:* Properly store all contract-related documents for future reference or auditing.

== Needs of Procurement & Contract Types 

=== Needs of Procurement
+ *Acquire Goods and Services:* Obtain materials, equipment, and services from external sources.
+ *Ensure Quality:* Select capable suppliers meeting quality standards.
+ *Control Costs:* Purchase at reasonable prices within the budget.
+ *Ensure Timely Delivery:* Make sure required resources are available when needed.
+ *Reduce Risks:* Mitigation of delays, poor quality, and supplier failure through solid contracts.
+ *Support Project Success:* Efficient execution of project deliverables.

=== Types of Contracts 
+ *Fixed-Price Contract:* The supplier agrees to provide goods or services for a fixed total price (*Example:* Purchasing 10 laptops for a fixed price).
+ *Cost-Reimbursable Contract:* The buyer pays the supplier for actual costs incurred plus an agreed fee or profit (*Example:* Hiring a consultant for expenses plus service fee).
+ *Time and Material Contract:* Payment is based on the time spent and materials used (*Example:* Hiring a software developer and paying hourly rates).
+ *Unit-Price Contract:* Payment is made according to the number of units delivered or work completed (*Example:* Paying per kilometer of road constructed).

=== Contract Statement of Work (SOW)
- A description of the work required for procurement (a type of scope statement).
- If used as part of a contract to describe only that contract's required work, it is called a *Contract Statement of Work*.
- Provides bidders a clear understanding of expectations.

== Enterprise Environmental Factors (EEF)

Enterprise Environmental Factors (EEF) are the internal and external conditions that influence or affect a project or organization, impacting planning, execution, and success.

=== Types of Enterprise Environmental Factors
+ *Internal Factors (Within the organization):*
  - Organizational structure
  - Company policies
  - Available resources
  - Employees and their skills
  - Organizational culture
+ *External Factors (Outside the organization):*
  - Market conditions
  - Government laws and regulations
  - Economic conditions
  - Technology changes
  - Competition
  - Political and social conditions

